Problem: A tower stands vertically on the ground. From a point on the ground, 30 m away from the foot of the tower, the angle of elevation of the top of the tower is 60^\circ. Find the height of the tower.

Solution:
Let height of tower = h meters.
Distance from base (Base) = 30 m.
Angle of elevation (\theta) = 60^\circ.
We know that \tan(\theta) = \frac{\text{Perpendicular}}{\text{Base}}
\tan(60^\circ) = \frac{h}{30}
Since \tan(60^\circ) = \sqrt{3}:
\sqrt{3} = \frac{h}{30}
h = 30\sqrt{3} \text{ meters (approx. 51.96 m)}
